course: everyday
lesson: 418
title: Sleep and school mornings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leo built a secret den under his duvet: tablet, brightness turned down, sound off, one eye on the door. He played Minecraft until midnight and felt like a spy. The next day in maths he fell asleep on his worksheet and woke up with «7 × 8» printed backwards on his cheek. Mrs Okafor phoned his mum that afternoon. The secret den did not last, and Leo found out that a tired brain in class costs more than the game ever gave.
explain: Other mornings went wrong too. Kai squeezed in «a quick Fortnite round» before school, missed the bus, and his mum had to drive him and was late for her own work. Ollie played late and snapped at his little sister over the cereal, then felt bad all day and said sorry after school, promising to go to bed earlier. Theo's eyes felt scratchy and dry after a late game, and he was grumpy until he slept more and played less. Ruby played right up to bedtime and dreamt all night that she was being chased by a giant pickaxe.
explain: Mr Bell, Noah's teacher, says he can tell who played late from the yawns: «Front row, three yawns, all before register. Fortnite or Roblox?» The class laughed, and Noah decided to play less on school nights. Children of nine and ten need about nine to twelve hours of sleep a night. An exciting game keeps the brain switched on, and a bright screen close to the face can make it harder to feel sleepy.
explain: The fixes were simple. Priya's family charges every device in the kitchen overnight; she called it annoying, then noticed she fell asleep faster. Zara set her console to switch off at eight on its own and found a book series she loved instead. Ruby stops games an hour before bed now, and the giant pickaxe has gone. Jayden told his squad his bedtime, and they moved the big 10 pm game to Saturday afternoons. Mia keeps long games for Friday and Saturday nights and says weekend games are more fun when you have waited for them.
fact: Children aged nine and ten need about nine to twelve hours of sleep a night.
fact: Too little sleep makes it hard to concentrate, learn and remember at school.
fact: Tired people are often grumpier and snap at others more easily.
fact: An exciting game keeps the brain alert, so it is harder to fall asleep straight after.
fact: Bright screens close to the face at night can make it harder to feel sleepy.
fact: Stopping screens about an hour before bed helps the brain calm down.
fact: Charging devices outside the bedroom removes the temptation to play at night.
fact: Many consoles and tablets have settings that switch them off at a set time, which helps when it is hard to make yourself stop.
fact: Games before school can make mornings rushed and make you late.
fact: Tired, dry or scratchy eyes are a sign that you need rest and sleep.
fact: Good friends will move a game to a time when everyone can play.
exercise: Why did Leo fall asleep in maths? | He had played on his tablet until midnight | His maths lesson was right after a big lunch | The classroom was too warm that day | He had stayed up reading a scary book
exercise: How did Leo feel while playing in his den? | Sneaky and excited like a spy | Scared and wanting to stop right away | Bored but still unable to get to sleep | Proud of finishing his homework
exercise: What happened after Leo fell asleep in class? | Mrs Okafor phoned his mum | He got a gold star for resting | Nobody in the class noticed at all | He was sent home for the day
exercise: What did Kai's game before school cost? | He missed the bus and his mum was late for work | He missed his lunch and his PE lesson that morning | He lost his best skin in the game | Nothing, he got to school early
exercise: How did Ollie's late night affect his morning? | He was grumpy with his little sister | He woke up early and full of energy | He forgot that it was a school day | He was too sleepy to eat breakfast
exercise: What did Ollie do about being grumpy? | Said sorry and promised to go to bed earlier | Blamed his sister for waking him up too early | Played even later the next night | Told his mum his sister started it
exercise: Why did Ruby dream about the game all night? | She played right up to bedtime | The game had a sleeping level | She was not tired at all that night | She listened to game music in bed
exercise: How much sleep do children of nine or ten need? | About nine to twelve hours a night | About five to six hours a night | About three to four hours a night | About fifteen to sixteen hours a night
exercise: Why is it hard to fall asleep straight after an exciting game? | The brain is still switched on | The body has run out of energy | The eyes have stopped working | The room has become too cold
exercise: Why does charging devices in the kitchen help Priya sleep? | The tablet is not there to tempt her | The kitchen makes the battery last longer | Her parents can play on it at night | The charger is too noisy in the bedroom
exercise: What did Zara do with her console settings? | Set it to switch off at eight on its own | Set it to play louder after eight | Set it to remind her to play more | Set it to stay on all through the night, every night
exercise: What did Zara find after her console switched off at eight? | A book series she loved | That she could not sleep at all | A way to switch it back on | That her friends stopped calling
exercise: How did Jayden solve the 10 pm squad problem? | He told them his bedtime and they moved it | He left the squad without saying anything | He played under the covers with no sound | He asked his mum for a later bedtime
exercise: Why are Mia's weekend games more fun? | She has waited all week for them | She plays different games at weekends | Her parents play with her at weekends | Weekend games give more coins
exercise: How can Noah's teacher tell who played late? | From all the yawning in class | From the game sounds on their phones | From their homework handwriting | From the time they arrive at school
exercise: What do Theo's scratchy eyes tell him? | That he needs rest and more sleep | That his screen needs to be brighter | That he should play in the dark | That he needs to play for longer
test: «I only play till midnight on school nights, it's fine, I'm not even tired». Which is true? | Too little sleep still makes school harder | If you don't feel tired you don't need sleep | Midnight is a normal bedtime for 9-year-olds | Games give the brain rest like sleep does
test: Mum: «why are you so snappy this morning?» Ollie: «dunno». What's the likely reason? | He played late and is tired | He ate far too much sugary cereal | His sister is always annoying | Mornings make everyone angry
test: «quick round before the bus, it's like 10 mins». What usually happens? | The round runs over and you're late | The bus waits for you to finish | You get to school calmer than usual | Your mum is glad you are busy
test: Priya: «phones in the kitchen at night?? that's prison rules». What did she find? | She fell asleep faster | She stayed awake all night | She played more the next day | She got more tired in the mornings
test: Squad at 10 pm: «you in or what? don't be a baby». Jayden's bedtime is 8:30. Best move? | Tell them his bedtime and suggest the weekend | Sneak online with the sound turned off and the light out | Say yes and blame his mum tomorrow | Quit the squad and never play again
test: Ruby: «I keep dreaming about the game, a giant pickaxe chased me». What helped her? | Stopping games an hour before bed | Playing the game just before sleeping | Sleeping with the tablet on her pillow | Turning the game music up louder
test: Teacher: «Three yawns before register. Fortnite or Roblox?» What does he mean? | Tired children show it the next day | He wants to play games with them | Yawning is a sign of being hungry | He thinks games make you sleepy
test: Zara: «my console turns off at 8 by itself, so sad». Why can that help? | An automatic rule helps when it's hard to stop yourself | It saves her from ever playing again | The console needs to rest at night or it wears out | Her friends are all offline by eight
test: Leo: «under the duvet nobody knows, brightness on 1». What's the problem? | He still loses sleep, and it shows next day | The brightness is too low to see properly | The tablet will get too hot and melt | Nobody will ever find out about it
test: Which is a sensible school-night plan? | Games after homework, screens off an hour before bed | Games until bedtime, then straight to sleep | Games in bed, so that you fall asleep while still playing | No sleep rule at all as long as it's fun
exam: State the quantity of sleep recommended nightly for children of nine to ten years of age. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately five to six hours | Approximately three to four hours | Approximately fifteen to sixteen hours
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the storage of electronic devices outside the bedroom overnight is advised. | It removes the temptation to play when sleep is required | It prolongs the life of the devices' batteries considerably | It permits parents to use the devices nocturnally | It prevents the devices from overheating
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of insufficient sleep in respect of a pupil's school day? | Concentration and mood are impaired | Learning is enhanced by the additional practice | No consequences arise if the pupil is not tired | Only physical education is affected
exam: It is given that a pupil's associates schedule an online game at 10 pm on school nights. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | To inform them of his bedtime and propose another time | To participate covertly with the sound disabled | To withdraw from the group without explanation | To participate and compensate by sleeping in class
